-- erwartet: cc
-- **646 -- a `section` name goes into C between quotes, and nothing escapes it.**
--
-- The `static` below declares a linker section whose name ends in a backslash. Gabbro's
-- `string` rule is *quote { char } quote* with *char = any character except quote and
-- newline* -- a backslash is an ordinary character there and means nothing. In C it means
-- the opposite, and the emitter copies the text through unchanged:
--
--     static uint64_t x __attribute__((section("a\"))) __attribute__((unused)) = 0;
--
-- The backslash escapes the closing quote, the string runs on, and `cc` says *missing
-- terminating " character*. The checker said `2 items, 0 errors, 0 hints`.
--
-- **The same slot has three further shapes, all measured on 2026-09-02 and all past the
-- checker**: an empty name and a blank one reach the ASSEMBLER, which answers *missing
-- name*; Gabbro's own doubling form for an embedded quote lands as *junk at end of line*;
-- and a section name holding a NUL draws *null character(s) preserved in literal*. **Two of
-- the four are found by the compiler and two only by the assembler**, which is why this file
-- carries a compiler-visible one: `tests/beispiele.rs` compiles a `-- erwartet: cc` probe
-- with `-fsyntax-only` and never assembles it.
--
-- > *That asymmetry is itself the finding one level up.* A gate that stops at
-- > `-fsyntax-only` cannot see half of what the emitter can write into a section name, and
-- > `instrumente/fuzze-erzeuger.py` compiles with `-c` for exactly this reason.

module gift::section_name_that_closes_the_c_string {

static mut x : u64 = 0 section "a\";

}
